{
  slug: 'the-case-for-a-redesign-of-wordpress-admin-a-professional-critique',
  title: 'The Case for a Redesign of WordPress Admin panel',
  date: '2024-12-19',
  sourceUrl: 'https://www.ryanjohnson.website/7913/',
  capturedAt: '2026-07-22',
  excerpt: 'The WordPress Admin interface is undeniably one of the most pivotal entry points into the WordPress ecosystem for millions of users and developers. However, despite its critical importance, it has long suffered from design issues that hinder productivity and usability. Below, I address some of the most pressing concerns and propose potential solutions to elevate [&hellip;]',
  contentHtml: '\n<p>The WordPress Admin interface is undeniably one of the most pivotal entry points into the WordPress ecosystem for millions of users and developers. However, despite its critical importance, it has long suffered from design issues that hinder productivity and usability. Below, I address some of the most pressing concerns and propose potential solutions to elevate the user experience.</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Disorganized Layout and Plugin Intrusion</h4>\n\n\n\n<p>One of the most glaring issues with the WordPress Admin area is the lack of organizational consistency. Plugins are allowed to modify almost every page in the admin interface, frequently inserting banners, notifications, or advertisements above the primary content. This clutter not only disrupts workflows but also undermines the professionalism of the platform.</p>\n\n\n\n<div id="block-551211-60eb-47" class="wp-block-gutenbee-tabs block-551211-60eb-47"><div class="wp-block-gutenbee-tabs-nav"><div class="wp-block-gutenbee-tabs-nav-item wp-block-gutenbee-tabs-nav-item-active">Page Clutter</div><div class="wp-block-gutenbee-tabs-nav-item">Home Page Editor</div><div class="wp-block-gutenbee-tabs-nav-item">Core Menu Items</div></div><div class="wp-block-gutenbee-tabs-tab-content-wrap"><div class="wp-block-gutenbee-tabs-tab-content" style="display:block"><p><img data-dominant-color="dcdbdb" data-has-transparency="false" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1000" height="668" class="wp-image-7935 not-transparent" style="--dominant-color: #dcdbdb; width: 1000px;" src="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Title-of-settings-page-is-inbetween-ads-png.avif" alt="" srcset="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Title-of-settings-page-is-inbetween-ads-png.avif 1515w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Title-of-settings-page-is-inbetween-ads-500x334.avif 500w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Title-of-settings-page-is-inbetween-ads-1024x684.avif 1024w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Title-of-settings-page-is-inbetween-ads-768x513.avif 768w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Title-of-settings-page-is-inbetween-ads-272x182.avif 272w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px" /></p></div><div class="wp-block-gutenbee-tabs-tab-content" style="display:none"><p><img data-dominant-color="857b77" data-has-transparency="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="300" height="270" class="wp-image-7936 has-transparency" style="--dominant-color: #857b77; width: 300px;" src="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/edit-front-page-menu-item-png.avif" alt=""><br>This could, and should, be called the Home Page. When someone upgrades to a block-based theme, the old Home page should be shown under the new home page so users can see that their work is not lost, and so they aren&#8217;t confused about where to find the new page to edit it. When you make a convention, then break your own convention&#8230;that&#8217;s a you problem.</p></div><div class="wp-block-gutenbee-tabs-tab-content" style="display:none"><p><img data-dominant-color="bdb8b8" data-has-transparency="false" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="600" height="374" class="wp-image-7939 not-transparent" style="--dominant-color: #bdb8b8; width: 600px;" src="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/posts-pages-part-1-1-png.avif" alt="" srcset="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/posts-pages-part-1-1-png.avif 681w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/posts-pages-part-1-1-500x312.avif 500w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px" /><br>Why is the Jetpack plugin settings link the first link in the side-bar? Seems a little stupid, no? Jetpack may be supported by WordPress&#8217;s team, but it shouldn&#8217;t supercede the core functionality of the WordPress software, should it?</p></div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<p>This is what the Side-Bar could look like with minor improvements. But don&#8217;t stop your imagination there.</p>\n\n\n\n<figure id="block-857ed2-c542-47" class="wp-block-gutenbee-image block-857ed2-c542-47 size-full"><img data-dominant-color="b6b4b3" data-has-transparency="false" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="682" height="271" src="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/posts-pages-part-2-png.avif" alt="" class="wp-image-7938 not-transparent" style="--dominant-color: #b6b4b3; box-shadow:7px 5px 23px 1px  rgba(0,0,0,0.5)" srcset="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/posts-pages-part-2-png.avif 682w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/posts-pages-part-2-500x199.avif 500w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 682px) 100vw, 682px" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>Proposed Solutions:</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<ol class="wp-block-list">\n<li><strong>Centralized Notification System:</strong> Introduce a notification center where all plugin messages, updates, and alerts are aggregated. This would prevent the constant visual interruptions caused by plugin banners on every page.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Scoped Page Modifications:</strong> Restrict plugins from modifying pages globally. Instead, allow them to add their features only within their designated settings or tool areas.</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Inconsistent Access Patterns for Plugin Settings</h4>\n\n\n\n<p>Navigating plugin settings often feels like a scavenger hunt. Different plugins employ varying access patterns, leading to a fragmented user experience. Some settings are buried within submenus, while others create their own top-level menu items, often without clear organization.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><img data-dominant-color="e1dfde" data-has-transparency="false" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="800" height="669" class="wp-image-7919 not-transparent" style="--dominant-color: #e1dfde; width: 800px;" src="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/side-bar-shit-highlighted-png.avif" alt="" srcset="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/side-bar-shit-highlighted-png.avif 1085w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/side-bar-shit-highlighted-500x418.avif 500w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/side-bar-shit-highlighted-1024x857.avif 1024w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/side-bar-shit-highlighted-768x643.avif 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px" /></p>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>Proposed Solutions:</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<ol class="wp-block-list">\n<li><strong>Standardized Plugin Menu Structure:</strong> Enforce guidelines that dictate where plugins can add their settings. For instance, group all plugin settings under a unified &#8220;Plugins&#8221; menu. A Plugins menu already exists, so start using it.<br><br><img data-dominant-color="b3aba9" data-has-transparency="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="400" height="398" class="wp-image-7918 has-transparency" style="--dominant-color: #b3aba9; width: 400px;" src="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/put-the-fucking-plugins-here-png.avif" alt="" srcset="https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/put-the-fucking-plugins-here-png.avif 378w, https://www.ryanjohnson.website/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/put-the-fucking-plugins-here-150x150.avif 150w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 400px) 100vw, 400px" /></li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Author-Based Grouping:</strong> Plugins created by the same author should be grouped together for better discoverability. This would also highlight interdependencies and potential conflicts. All plugins and vendors should be organized based on their level of legitimacy:\n<ul class="wp-block-list">\n<li>Official plugins from WordPress</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Plugins from reputable vendors\n<ul class="wp-block-list">\n<li>Tag these plugins based on whether each plugin &#8220;has support&#8221; or &#8220;has no support&#8221;</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Plugins that have no reputation and no support</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Misplacement of Core Menu Items</h4>\n\n\n\n<p>The default order of menu items, such as placing “Media” and “Posts” before “Pages,” reflects a misunderstanding of the hierarchy of content. Conceptually, pages serve as the foundation of a website, while posts are a subtype of pages.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>Proposed Solution:</strong> Reorder menu items to reflect logical hierarchies: Pages first, followed by their subtypes (e.g., Posts). This would align the interface with how most users conceptualize website content.</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Plugin Conflicts and Implicit Behaviors</h4>\n\n\n\n<p>Conflicts between plugins are a recurring problem. For example, when two plugins from the same developer are installed, one may deactivate or override the other without clear communication. This implicit behavior is confusing and frustrating for users.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>Proposed Solutions:</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<ol class="wp-block-list">\n<li><strong>Conflict Notification:</strong> When a plugin disables another, provide a clear notification explaining the conflict and its cause.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Grouped Display:</strong> Plugins from the same developer should appear grouped together in the plugin list, with clear indications of interdependencies or conflicts.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Disabled Plugin Visibility:</strong> Even when disabled, plugins should remain visible in the list but greyed out, with notes explaining their status and the responsible plugin.</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Addressing Advertisements and Spam</h4>\n\n\n\n<p>The current handling of plugin-generated advertisements and spam messages is another major pain point. These messages often dominate the field of view, detracting from the primary purpose of the admin interface.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><strong>Proposed Solutions:</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<ol class="wp-block-list">\n<li><strong>Notification Center for Ads:</strong> Relocate all plugin advertisements and promotional messages to the aforementioned centralized notification system.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Opt-Out Mechanisms:</strong> Allow users to opt out of non-critical notifications from plugins.</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<h4 class="wp-block-heading">A Call to Action</h4>\n\n\n\n<p>The WordPress Admin interface has remained largely unchanged through several overhauls, perpetuating these long-standing issues. As a community driven by innovation and usability, it’s time for a comprehensive redesign. Addressing these challenges would not only enhance productivity for existing users but also improve the platform’s appeal to new adopters.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>By prioritizing logical organization, consistency, and a user-first approach, WordPress can maintain its position as a leading content management system while setting a new standard for admin interface design. Let’s make this drop-dead obvious improvement a reality.</p>\n\n\n\n<p><em>Note: This post was generated by AI and modified with additional information</em></p>\n',
  wordpressId: 7913,
}
